<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/intermediate/magnetism-and-induction">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Magnetism is what electric charge does when it <strong>moves</strong>. A current makes a
    magnetic field (Ørsted, 1820 — compass needles twitch near wires); a magnetic field pushes
    moving charges sideways; and a <em>changing</em> magnetic field creates voltage from nothing
    but motion (Faraday, 1831). Those three facts generate every motor, transformer and power
    station on Earth, and they are all one subject: electricity and magnetism, separate for two
    thousand years, unified in a single laboratory decade. This chapter walks the chain from
    fridge magnets to the grid — poles and fields first, then the three great machines
    (electromagnet, motor, generator), then the transformer that stitches them into the
    national infrastructure.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Faraday's law, step by step — the chain from moving magnet to the grid" widget="equationStep" config={faradayCfg} />

  <h2>Magnets and magnetic fields</h2>
  <p>
    Every magnet has two poles, north-seeking and south-seeking, and the first iron rule of
    the subject is that <strong>poles come in pairs</strong>: cut a magnet in half and you get
    two complete magnets, never an isolated pole. Like poles repel, unlike attract, and the
    invisible structure between them is described by <strong>field lines</strong> running from
    north pole to south pole (outside the magnet), bunching where the field is strong. Only a
    few metals are magnetic at all — iron, steel (iron plus carbon), nickel, cobalt — and they
    divide into <strong>soft magnetic</strong> materials (pure iron: magnetises easily, loses
    it easily — electromagnet cores) and <strong>hard</strong> ones (steel, alnico, neodymium
    alloys: keep their alignment — permanent magnets). The microscopic picture: each iron
    atom is a tiny magnet (a quantum property of its electrons, not a literal bar), atoms team
    up in <strong>domains</strong>, and magnetising a material just recruits more domains into
    one direction. Earth itself is a giant dipole — its core's churning liquid iron acts as a
    self-sustaining dynamo — and a compass needle's "north-seeking" pole points north because
    the geographic north actually sits near a magnetic <em>south</em> pole (opposites
    attract): the naming is a convention, the attraction is physics. The field also protects
    the planet, deflecting the solar wind along the poles — the auroras are that traffic
    visible.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> magnets attract all metals. They attract only iron,
  steel, nickel and cobalt (and a few rare alloys); aluminium, copper, brass and gold are
  unmoved — the reason a scrapyard crane lifts cars (steel) but the copper in the same car
  needs separate recovery. A related error: fridge magnets stick because of the magnet,
  full stop — actually the steel door is induced into an opposite-pole layer, and the magnet
  holds itself to its own creation.</p>

  <h2>Current makes magnetism</h2>
  <p>
    Ørsted's 1820 lecture accident (a compass twitched beside a live wire) opened the door:
    <strong>every current is wrapped in circular magnetic field lines</strong>, clockwise or
    anticlockwise depending on the current's direction (right-hand grip rule: thumb along the
    current, fingers curl the way the field goes). Coil the wire into a <strong>solenoid</strong>
    and the loops' fields add into a bar-magnet pattern with a controllable strength — scale
    with the current, the number of turns, and above all the core: slide an iron bar in and
    the field strengthens by a factor of hundreds to thousands. That is the
    <strong>electromagnet</strong>: a magnet with an on-switch, and its applications are the
    modern world's hidden furniture — scrapyard cranes, relays and door buzzers, starter
    motors, Maglev rails, particle-accelerator magnets, and MRI scanners whose 1.5–3 tesla
    fields are some 30,000 times Earth's. Pause on the asymmetry this created: electric
    charge exists alone (a rubbed rod), but magnetic poles never do — a mystery that would
    take Maxwell to explain properly and remains true today.
  </p>

  <h2>Fields push moving charge: motors</h2>
  <p>
    Reverse the logic and the field pushes back on any current you supply: a wire of length L
    carrying current I across a field B feels a sideways force <strong>F = B·I·L</strong>,
    direction given by Fleming's left-hand rule (field, current, force — thumb, first finger,
    second finger). Worked example: a 10 cm wire carrying 1 A across a 0.5 T field feels
    F = 0.5 × 1 × 0.1 = <strong>0.05 N</strong> — small, but put thousands of wire-turns on a
    rotor and it turns into torque. The <strong>d.c. motor</strong> is exactly this force
    arranged to rotate: a current loop in a field feels opposite forces on its two sides, a
    couple that spins the loop — and the clever part is the <strong>split-ring
    commutator</strong>, which reverses the current through the loop every half-turn so the
    torque never changes sign. Loudspeakers use the same law in and out: the coil's force
    oscillates a cone at the signal's frequency, pushing air into sound (the microphone is
    the same device run backwards — induction, below).
  </p>

  <h2>Changing fields make voltage: induction</h2>
  <p>
    Eleven years after Ørsted, Faraday closed the loop the other way. His law: only a
    <strong>changing</strong> magnetic flux through a circuit induces an emf — flux Φ being
    field strength times the loop's area (count the field lines threading the loop), and the
    induced emf being <strong>EMF = −N·ΔΦ/Δt</strong>: more turns, faster change, more
    voltage. A motionless magnet in a motionless coil does nothing, however strong — the
    widget's steps walk through it. The minus sign is <strong>Lenz's law</strong>, and it is
    energy conservation in disguise: the induced current flows so as to oppose the change
    that made it — push a magnet toward a coil and the coil pushes back; pull it away and it
    tugs. Without that opposition, dropping a magnet into a coil would generate unlimited
    free energy; with it, the work you do against the magnetic resistance is exactly the
    electrical energy you get out. (Drop a magnet down a copper tube and watch it fall in
    slow motion: the induced currents' opposing fields brake it — the demo of Lenz in real
    time.)
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>generator</strong> is induction industrialised. Spin a coil in a magnetic
    field and the flux swings sinusoidally through it, inducing an alternating emf — sine
    waves at the rotation frequency, which is literally what mains electricity is (UK: 50 Hz;
    Americas: 60 Hz; the grid is a fleet of Faraday machines phase-locked together).
    <strong>Every large power station on Earth except solar PV</strong> — coal, gas, nuclear,
    hydro, wind — is this machine: a turbine spinning coils in fields, differing only in
    what turns the shaft. Slip rings deliver the AC out (a commutator, reversing instead,
    would give DC — that's a motor's trick run in reverse).
  </p>

  <h2>The transformer: voltage without moving parts</h2>
  <p>
    Put two coils on a shared iron core and AC in the first coil drives changing flux through
    the second, inducing an emf — with the voltages in the ratio of the turns:
    <strong>V₂/V₁ = N₂/N₁</strong>. Worked example: a phone charger stepping 230 V mains down
    to 12 V needs a turns ratio of about 1150 : 60 (≈ 19 : 1). Power is (nearly) conserved —
    a 99%-efficient transformer passing 100 W steps voltage up and current down in the same
    proportion — so a transformer is a voltage gear lever, not an energy source. This is the
    grid's master trick: generate at ~25 kV, step up to 400 kV for transmission (cutting
    I²R losses to a fraction of a percent, as the circuits chapter showed), step down
    regionally, then to 230 V at your street. Only <em>alternating</em> current works — a
    steady current makes no changing flux — which is why the 1890s AC/DC "war of the
    currents" ended as it did, and why modern HVDC schemes (which do transmit DC) need
    expensive power electronics to play in transformers' territory.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Maxwell's synthesis: light as electromagnetism.</strong> Maxwell's 1861–65
    equations completed the picture by adding one more symmetry: a <em>changing electric
    field</em> also makes a magnetic field. With that, the two fields could regenerate each
    other and fly off as a self-sustaining wave — and computing the wave's speed from
    laboratory constants on electricity and magnetism alone gave 3 × 10⁸ m/s: the measured
    speed of light. Light, Maxwell realised, <em>is</em> an electromagnetic wave — the
    spectrum from radio to gamma (the Waves chapter's family) being the same knot of
    oscillating fields at different frequencies. Ørsted's twitching compass had, within two
    generations, explained the rainbow.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>RMS: why "230 V" is a sine wave's nickname.</strong> A generator's output swings
    between +325 V and −325 V (for UK mains), yet we quote 230 V. The quoted figure is the
    <strong>root-mean-square</strong>: the DC voltage that would deliver the same heating
    power, computed by averaging the squared values over a cycle and taking the square root —
    for a sine, V_rms = V_peak/√2. The choice is honest: your kettle cares about heating
    (power ∝ V²), and the RMS value makes AC and DC bills comparable. The same convention
    sets every voltmeter's AC dial, audio engineering's wattage ratings, and the safety
    margins of insulation — all calibrated to the average of the square, because energy
    cares about the square.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Eddy currents: induction where you don't want it — and where you do.</strong>
    Bulk conductors in changing fields develop swirling induced currents that waste power
    as heat (transformer cores are laminated — sliced into insulated sheets — precisely to
    strangle them). Harness the same currents and you get the induction hob (a rapidly
    alternating field heats the pan's base directly — no element), induction braking on
    rollercoasters and trucks (frictionless, fail-safe, stronger the faster you go), and
    metal detectors (the coil reads the back-reaction of the target's induced currents).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Back-emf: motors have opinions.</strong> A spinning motor coil is also a
    generator: its rotation through the field induces an emf that <em>opposes</em> the
    supply (Lenz again). At speed, back-emf nearly cancels the supply, so the motor draws
    only modest current; stall it (hold the shaft) and back-emf vanishes — current leaps to
    the coil's Ohm's-law limit and the windings cook. This is why electric trains "run
    down" gracefully and why a jammed drill smells hot within seconds: the motor's own
    induction is its speed governor.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions and the quantum floor.</strong> Magnetic monopoles — isolated
    north or south poles — have been sought since Dirac argued quantum mechanics almost
    demands them; none has ever been confirmed (the field, in both senses, remains open).
    And the origin of every permanent magnet is quantum: electron <em>spin</em>, a property
    with no classical analogue, makes each electron a microscopic dipole; ferromagnetism is
    a conspiracy of spins, and its theory (like high-temperature superconductivity's)
    remains one of physics' unfinished grand projects. Meanwhile Earth's own dipole
    <em>reverses</em> every few hundred thousand years — the rock record shows the poles
    wandering and flipping — a reminder that the "permanent" magnetism holding your
    shopping list to the fridge is a dynamic, churned planetary process on the long view.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Ørsted (1820); Faraday, Experimental Researches in Electricity (1831); Maxwell, "On Physical Lines of Force" (1861); OpenStax, College Physics (ch. 22–23, Magnetism and Electromagnetic Induction); BGS geomagnetism resources (reversal record).</p>
</TopicPage>
